1
00:00:06,066 --> 00:00:07,500
[keys jangling]

2
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:16,666
[engine starts]

3
00:00:22,666 --> 00:00:25,566
[engine revving]

4
00:00:31,299 --> 00:00:35,833
[Jerry] This is the 1976
Lamborghini Countach LP400,

5
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,699
in Tahiti Blue with a tan interior.

6
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,533
It has a 370 horsepower, four liter,

7
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,500
four cam, 12 cylinder.

8
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:49,099
The fuel comes through six
twin-throat Webers.

9
00:00:49,166 --> 00:00:50,500
That is good.

10
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,500
And, it could top out 
at 180 miles per hour,

11
00:00:55,566 --> 00:00:57,900
which, in 1976...

12
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:01,199
worked.

13
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:04,866
I love the short, sharp front end to it.

14
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:07,300
It's just like a little knife blade
through the air.

15
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:11,300
The idea of this car, I think,
is you pull up in it,

16
00:01:11,366 --> 00:01:13,266
and you are taking over the scene.

17
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:14,566
Not unlike my guest today,

18
00:01:14,633 --> 00:01:18,233
the amazingly versatile
and visually spectacular Jim Carrey.

19
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:22,066
Jim is an unforgettably gifted comedian
and comic actor.

20
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:24,700
Can you imagine anybody
else even trying to do

21
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:27,887
Ace Ventura, The Mask,
or Dumb and Dumber? Forget it!

22
00:01:28,321 --> 00:01:29,756
Don't even make the movies.

23
00:01:32,833 --> 00:01:35,133
-[Jim] Yeah?
-[Jerry] Jimmy, it's Jerry.

24
00:01:35,199 --> 00:01:37,533
[Jim] Jerry? [gasps]
What time is it?

25
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:40,033
[Jerry] Remember, we talked about
going for a cup of coffee.

26
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,866
[Jim] Oh, God, is that today? 
Is this today?

27
00:01:41,933 --> 00:01:44,899
[Jerry] Of course today is today!
Today is always today.

28
00:01:44,938 --> 00:01:47,540
[Jim] Yeah, no, no, no.
I'm good. I sleep in my clothes.

29
00:01:47,607 --> 00:01:48,708
I'll be right out.

30
00:01:52,866 --> 00:01:54,166
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, 

31
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:57,600
and this is 
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

32
00:02:01,154 --> 00:02:02,455
[door ringing]

33
00:02:04,791 --> 00:02:07,293
-[Jim grunting]
-[Jerry laughing]

34
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,799
-Doesn't the door work?
-Oh, yeah. It works.

35
00:02:17,233 --> 00:02:18,533
-Whew.
-Is that Don Knotts?

36
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:21,666
Did you ever think about
the Barney Fife sniff?

37
00:02:21,799 --> 00:02:22,866
[both sniffling]

38
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:24,633
Oh, yeah.

39
00:02:24,777 --> 00:02:26,579
-Getting ready.
-That was the whole character,

40
00:02:26,666 --> 00:02:28,500
-wasn't it?
-Getting ready to tell you who he is.

41
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:30,733
[Jim] This is beautiful.

42
00:02:30,799 --> 00:02:34,133
[Jerry] 1976 Lamborghini Countach.

43
00:02:35,033 --> 00:02:38,066
They called it that because
that was what people exclaimed.

44
00:02:38,133 --> 00:02:40,933
When you blow past them in traffic,
"Countach!"

45
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:49,002
[Jim] Ooh, baby!

46
00:02:49,168 --> 00:02:50,537
Oh, yeah, baby.

47
00:02:50,603 --> 00:02:52,005
Woo-hoo.

48
00:02:52,071 --> 00:02:53,740
It does feel like a Bond movie,
doesn't it?

49
00:02:53,833 --> 00:02:54,966
It's fantastic.

50
00:02:57,533 --> 00:03:00,066
Barney Fife was ego
with nothing to back it up,

51
00:03:00,133 --> 00:03:01,833
and I always think that's the funniest--

52
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:03,333
-The funniest.
-...kind of character.

53
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:07,299
And yet, somehow, that confidence
and that arrogance wins in the end.

54
00:03:07,366 --> 00:03:10,233
[Jerry] Who else, for you,
was in a special category?

55
00:03:10,299 --> 00:03:12,866
[Jim] I love David Letterman.
He's big in my life.

56
00:03:12,933 --> 00:03:15,333
It's so funny
because he's such a crank, too.

57
00:03:15,399 --> 00:03:18,433
I think it was the second,
maybe third time I was on,

58
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:20,200
I hadn't been back there
for quite a while,

59
00:03:20,266 --> 00:03:22,466
maybe two-and-a-half years
or something like that.

60
00:03:22,533 --> 00:03:25,133
During the commercial,
he leaned over to me

61
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,333
after we'd just had the most

62
00:03:26,399 --> 00:03:29,166
incredibly wonderful time together
on camera,

63
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:31,100
he leaned over to me, and he said,

64
00:03:31,166 --> 00:03:33,200
"So, why haven't you been here
in three years?

65
00:03:33,266 --> 00:03:35,666
Is it me? Is it me?"

66
00:03:36,849 --> 00:03:40,486
And then, "We're back!"
So, I was like, "Yeah..."

67
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:42,500
And he was just back to being Dave.

68
00:03:42,566 --> 00:03:44,566
-"Is it me?"
-"Is it me?"

69
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:46,233
"Well, who else could it be?"

70
00:03:46,299 --> 00:03:47,299
Exactly.

71
00:03:47,366 --> 00:03:48,766
It's not Biff.

72
00:03:53,899 --> 00:03:56,133
Did they have air conditioning in '76?

73
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:57,533
[Jerry] They do. It's on.

74
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:00,600
But it's kind of like an Alaskan husky
breathing on you.

75
00:04:00,666 --> 00:04:01,766
Okay.

76
00:04:06,866 --> 00:04:08,233
Get me out!

77
00:04:08,333 --> 00:04:09,399
Ah.

78
00:04:14,799 --> 00:04:16,333
[chuckles]

79
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:18,000
Just knocking on the fourth wall there.

80
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:21,099
Amazing. It really is real.

81
00:04:25,266 --> 00:04:27,099
Hey, how you doing, man?

82
00:04:27,166 --> 00:04:29,166
You wanna go? You wanna go?

83
00:04:29,233 --> 00:04:31,000
Blow your doors off in this thing.

84
00:04:34,507 --> 00:04:37,110
-[Jim] I had a McLaren for a little while.
-[Jerry] You did?

85
00:04:37,176 --> 00:04:40,847
Yeah. It was too fast for me.
That thing was like [gulps].

86
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:42,933
And you realize, "What am I eating?"

87
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,200
And it's your testicles.

88
00:04:44,266 --> 00:04:47,366
I'm driving a Tesla. I love the Tesla.

89
00:04:47,433 --> 00:04:49,000
See, I like burning stuff.

90
00:04:49,055 --> 00:04:51,291
We love breathing
what you're burning, baby.

91
00:04:55,828 --> 00:04:58,364
I'm fairly non-localized these days.

92
00:04:58,433 --> 00:05:01,233
So, non-localized means,
it doesn't matter where you are.

93
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:03,033
-I'm everywhere.
-You're everywhere.

94
00:05:08,566 --> 00:05:11,300
Jerry, you are full of testosterone.

95
00:05:13,733 --> 00:05:15,766
I've been on a cleanse for 21 days.

96
00:05:15,833 --> 00:05:16,833
-Really?
-Yeah.

97
00:05:16,883 --> 00:05:19,018
This is my 21st day of a cleanse.

98
00:05:19,619 --> 00:05:22,255
It's incredible.
Did you just see me climb that gate?

99
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:25,525
You should have seen me
on the other side, though. I was like...

100
00:05:26,599 --> 00:05:27,766
[grunting]

101
00:05:27,966 --> 00:05:30,333
You were like a dog
trying to get out of a pool.

102
00:05:32,166 --> 00:05:35,000
That'll be you on the 22nd day
of the cleanse, right there.

103
00:05:35,068 --> 00:05:39,238
That's where I'm headed, and I'm about
to go up north to Marin County

104
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:41,700
and do a silent retreat for five days,

105
00:05:41,766 --> 00:05:44,533
so I won't be speaking during the film.
I don't speak.

106
00:05:44,599 --> 00:05:45,966
You are perfect for that.

107
00:05:46,033 --> 00:05:48,666
You're one of the few guys
who is not handicapped at all...

108
00:05:48,733 --> 00:05:50,400
-No.
-...by not speaking.

109
00:05:55,233 --> 00:05:57,233
[Jerry] I like to remember
people's old bits.

110
00:05:57,300 --> 00:05:58,666
I remember your Henry Fonda

111
00:05:58,733 --> 00:06:00,599
-was one of my favorite bits.
-On Golden Pond.

112
00:06:00,666 --> 00:06:02,633
"Bottom line, huh!"

113
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:05,333
-"You're a bottom line, man."
-"You're a bottom line, man."

114
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:06,900
"You wanna see my teeth?"

115
00:06:08,833 --> 00:06:12,599
Have you noticed that orange juice
now comes in a variety of pulp?

116
00:06:12,666 --> 00:06:14,733
Like subtle pulp, middle pulp...

117
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:17,533
You can't take pulp. I need more pulp.

118
00:06:17,599 --> 00:06:20,133
[Jim] You know about the relative
and the absolute, right?

119
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:24,266
The relative is the things you relate to,
and the absolute is a truth

120
00:06:24,333 --> 00:06:26,666
of consciousness being everything.

121
00:06:30,133 --> 00:06:33,200
So, relating that to the three
different levels of pulp.

122
00:06:33,266 --> 00:06:35,333
No pulp, some pulp...

123
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:36,833
-There is no pulp.
-...or extra pulp.

124
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:39,666
There is no pulp no matter how much pulp
they tell you there is.

125
00:06:39,732 --> 00:06:42,368
Pulp is an illusion. There is sensations--

126
00:06:42,468 --> 00:06:44,871
What are those little bits in the juice?

127
00:06:44,966 --> 00:06:46,233
I feel them.

128
00:06:49,333 --> 00:06:50,566
[Jerry] We're in a marina.

129
00:06:50,633 --> 00:06:52,366
This is very refreshing.

130
00:06:52,433 --> 00:06:53,500
[Jim] This is fantastic.

131
00:06:53,566 --> 00:06:55,833
I don't know
how to get out of this, but...

132
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:56,866
[Jerry chuckles]

133
00:06:57,299 --> 00:06:59,533
Oh, there it is. [whistles]

134
00:06:59,599 --> 00:07:02,299
It slices, it dices, it juliennes fries.

135
00:07:02,355 --> 00:07:04,424
Anybody got some celery, or some...

136
00:07:05,133 --> 00:07:07,066
stuff they need chopped?

137
00:07:07,133 --> 00:07:09,466
[inaudible]

138
00:07:09,662 --> 00:07:11,631
-[vehicle beeping]
-Wait, we should be backing up

139
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:13,266
while that sound is happening.

140
00:07:18,671 --> 00:07:20,306
[inaudible]

141
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:23,833
[inaudible]

142
00:07:24,911 --> 00:07:26,679
-How you doing, officer?
-Good. How are you?

143
00:07:26,766 --> 00:07:27,900
I'm excellent, man.

144
00:07:37,433 --> 00:07:38,700
[waitress] Huevos rancheros?

145
00:07:38,766 --> 00:07:40,333
That sounds good. Okay, I'll have that.

146
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:43,233
Sure, kill yourself. It's the Killer Cafe.

147
00:07:46,633 --> 00:07:48,933
So, this is how
I keep life interesting, Jerry.

148
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:50,333
I bring my own sweetener.

149
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:52,599
Look, if you do this,

150
00:07:52,666 --> 00:07:53,766
if you go up here,

151
00:07:53,833 --> 00:07:56,533
and then you see how far you can do--

152
00:07:56,599 --> 00:07:58,033
This could be fantastic.

153
00:07:58,099 --> 00:07:59,433
Just estimating now.

154
00:07:59,933 --> 00:08:01,666
I got it. First time.

155
00:08:01,733 --> 00:08:03,266
-Three.
-[Jerry] Oh, my God.

156
00:08:03,333 --> 00:08:04,633
[Jim] Five. Boom.

157
00:08:05,466 --> 00:08:06,533
Thank you.

158
00:08:10,957 --> 00:08:13,459
I used to watch you at the Improv
when I was starting out.

159
00:08:13,533 --> 00:08:14,966
You made me laugh so hard

160
00:08:15,033 --> 00:08:18,000
because I just started
learning to scuba dive.

161
00:08:18,066 --> 00:08:19,666
You did that bit about scuba diving,

162
00:08:19,732 --> 00:08:22,268
and how the whole point of the sport
is not to die.

163
00:08:22,401 --> 00:08:24,237
There's a fish, a rock.
Who cares? Don't die.

164
00:08:24,333 --> 00:08:26,566
♪ I don't wanna die
Don't let me die ♪

165
00:08:26,633 --> 00:08:28,200
Tell me about your stand-up.

166
00:08:28,266 --> 00:08:30,333
You know, first I did impressions.

167
00:08:30,376 --> 00:08:33,546
Lot of people start out with impressions,
but I was obsessed with impressions.

168
00:08:33,633 --> 00:08:34,933
And when I started out,

169
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,866
-I was a Canadian.
-"Was"?

170
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:37,966
I am, is.

171
00:08:38,033 --> 00:08:41,133
I sent a letter to Carol Burnett
when I was ten years old.

172
00:08:41,199 --> 00:08:43,799
"Dear Carol, I do 120 people.

173
00:08:43,866 --> 00:08:46,366
I would really like
to be a regular on your show,

174
00:08:46,433 --> 00:08:47,900
like Tim Conway and Harvey Korman."

175
00:08:47,966 --> 00:08:50,300
-At ten? That's great.
-Yeah. At ten.

176
00:08:50,366 --> 00:08:51,866
I got a letter back in the mail,

177
00:08:51,933 --> 00:08:54,233
and it was, of course,
one of the assistants, or whatever.

178
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:56,733
"We wish you the best of luck,
sounds like you're doing great."

179
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:59,133
I was so in heaven.

180
00:08:59,199 --> 00:09:01,600
I got a letter from Carol Burnett.

181
00:09:01,666 --> 00:09:04,366
And it had Television City crest on it.

182
00:09:04,433 --> 00:09:06,800
I'd struck the big time. I'd made it.

183
00:09:06,866 --> 00:09:08,400
I got a letter from Hollywood.

184
00:09:12,266 --> 00:09:15,366
In Living Color was...
I'm terrible with dates.

185
00:09:15,433 --> 00:09:16,666
I love the fireman.

186
00:09:16,733 --> 00:09:18,033
Yeah, the fire marshal, Bill.

187
00:09:18,090 --> 00:09:22,028
The burn victim fire department guy
was the guy that made us.

188
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:24,633
When you were really in stand-up days,

189
00:09:24,699 --> 00:09:25,933
what was your set like?

190
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,633
Well, at first, when I decided not to do
the impressions anymore,

191
00:09:28,699 --> 00:09:31,066
I called it performance art,
because it wasn't funny.

192
00:09:31,133 --> 00:09:32,866
But you just cut it, the whole--

193
00:09:32,933 --> 00:09:34,600
Cut it out. In one shot.

194
00:09:34,666 --> 00:09:35,866
And what were you doing then?

195
00:09:35,933 --> 00:09:39,066
I was doing, like, uh,
15 minutes as a cockroach

196
00:09:39,133 --> 00:09:41,233
trying to avoid the vacuum cleaner.

197
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:44,300
He knows that there's a couple spots
in the living room he can go.

198
00:09:44,350 --> 00:09:47,486
There's the baseboard over there.
Over there, there's something else.

199
00:09:47,566 --> 00:09:50,066
I used to do Jesus,

200
00:09:50,133 --> 00:09:53,033
and if that were me, the Bible
would be a whole different book.

201
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:55,500
You know, there'd be a scene
where I'm on the cross,

202
00:09:55,566 --> 00:09:57,699
saying, "Wait till my father
hears about this.

203
00:09:57,766 --> 00:09:59,166
You guys are going to get it!"

204
00:09:59,233 --> 00:10:01,366
Kids say "You guys are
going to get it" a lot.

205
00:10:01,433 --> 00:10:02,766
"You guys are going to get it!"

206
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:06,966
-What is your name?
-Molly.

207
00:10:07,033 --> 00:10:08,266
I'm sorry. I should have asked.

208
00:10:08,333 --> 00:10:10,566
-[Molly] It's okay.
-It's not really who you are, anyway.

209
00:10:10,633 --> 00:10:11,733
I feel like a Molly, though.

210
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:13,033
-Yeah?
-Yeah.

211
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:14,466
You'll find out different.

212
00:10:14,533 --> 00:10:17,133
Thank you very much, Molly.
Molly, for now.

213
00:10:17,199 --> 00:10:19,433
-Thank you, Molly, for now.
-[Molly giggles]

214
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,733
And I break it down all the time,
philosophically, about comedy.

215
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:30,166
People come here
to feel good about themselves.

216
00:10:30,233 --> 00:10:32,966
And a laugh is actually

217
00:10:33,033 --> 00:10:35,100
a feeling of superiority to something.

218
00:10:35,166 --> 00:10:37,566
So, there has to be a childlike fun to it.

219
00:10:37,633 --> 00:10:39,633
You can be angry and smarter than us,

220
00:10:39,699 --> 00:10:41,166
but make it fun for us.

221
00:10:42,699 --> 00:10:45,033
I still get caught up in the small me
every once in a while.

222
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:47,333
Once or twice a day,
I'll get caught up in, like,

223
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:48,833
"My God, this is...

224
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:50,333
If that guy doesn't...

225
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:52,300
How could they do that to the small me?"

226
00:10:52,366 --> 00:10:53,900
Because that's in everybody.

227
00:10:53,966 --> 00:10:55,199
But they don't know it.

228
00:10:55,266 --> 00:10:57,966
They don't know that at the end,
they're going to be so relieved.

229
00:10:58,033 --> 00:11:00,766
Death is going to be
such a welcome freaking moment,

230
00:11:00,833 --> 00:11:03,966
because they've been trying
so freaking hard,

231
00:11:04,033 --> 00:11:05,166
and that's the feeling.

232
00:11:05,233 --> 00:11:07,400
And I can get there sometimes now.

233
00:11:07,466 --> 00:11:10,133
I'm gonna need
one more cup of coffee to get there.

234
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:16,966
Did you meet Andy Kaufman?

235
00:11:17,033 --> 00:11:18,466
Andy, I knew a little bit.

236
00:11:18,533 --> 00:11:19,933
Like anybody knows Andy.

237
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,000
-Yeah.
-Right?

238
00:11:21,066 --> 00:11:22,233
I love that movie.

239
00:11:22,566 --> 00:11:24,800
[Jim] I mean, honest to God,
that was like a possession.

240
00:11:24,866 --> 00:11:27,800
And once I decided that,
all hell broke loose.

241
00:11:27,933 --> 00:11:29,733
I remember before that movie started,

242
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:32,133
my friend Nic Cage and me went to dinner.

243
00:11:32,199 --> 00:11:35,366
Nic said, "I gotta go.

244
00:11:35,433 --> 00:11:36,733
Your eyes are different.

245
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:38,199
I don't know who you are."

246
00:11:38,266 --> 00:11:40,533
It got really uncomfortable.

247
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:42,300
It's hard to make Nic Cage uncomfortable.

248
00:11:42,366 --> 00:11:43,466
Hard to weird him out.

249
00:11:43,533 --> 00:11:44,900
Hard to weird Nic out.

250
00:11:48,733 --> 00:11:49,866
Thank you so much, Molly.

251
00:11:49,933 --> 00:11:51,166
-No, I insist.
-Thank you.

252
00:11:51,233 --> 00:11:53,833
"I insist." That word really
backs people up, doesn't it?

253
00:11:54,033 --> 00:11:57,100
"You should pay your taxes."
"No, I insist that I do not!"

254
00:11:57,166 --> 00:11:59,033
Do you want to try the Stevia
on the floor?

255
00:11:59,100 --> 00:12:00,433
Yeah, I'll just go for it.

256
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:01,500
This is fun, Jerry.

257
00:12:01,566 --> 00:12:03,266
[Jerry] Why not?
Why shouldn't we have fun?

258
00:12:03,366 --> 00:12:04,900
It used to be like that with my father.

259
00:12:04,966 --> 00:12:07,800
Rodney Dangerfield
totally fell in love with my dad.

260
00:12:07,866 --> 00:12:11,066
My dad comes to see me.
I introduce him to Rodney.

261
00:12:11,133 --> 00:12:13,733
I'm playing with Rodney, and I get stuck,

262
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:15,633
and I go like, "Tag team comedy,"

263
00:12:15,699 --> 00:12:17,166
like that, and my dad tags me,

264
00:12:17,233 --> 00:12:19,000
comes in, and starts peppering him.

265
00:12:19,066 --> 00:12:21,566
Rodney's like, "Who the hell is this guy?"

266
00:12:21,633 --> 00:12:24,066
He's laughing hysterically at my dad,

267
00:12:24,133 --> 00:12:26,900
and then started
calling him on his birthday,

268
00:12:26,966 --> 00:12:28,500
and, "How's Percy doing, man?

269
00:12:28,566 --> 00:12:31,000
That guy, man,
he's the funniest guy I ever saw!"

270
00:12:31,699 --> 00:12:32,933
[Jerry] All right.

271
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,699
I'm gonna try as best I can.

272
00:12:35,766 --> 00:12:37,533
I'm gonna go for the record now.

273
00:12:39,866 --> 00:12:42,333
[dramatic music playing]

274
00:12:49,533 --> 00:12:50,800
That's amazing!

275
00:12:50,866 --> 00:12:53,333
You know, it doesn't take much

276
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:54,833
to feel good about yourself.

277
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:56,966
There's nothing less than that.

278
00:12:57,033 --> 00:12:58,033
Cheers.

279
00:12:58,099 --> 00:13:00,266
[applauding]

280
00:13:05,184 --> 00:13:08,754
-What are you doing other than this?
-Usually, every weekend, I will go out...

281
00:13:08,833 --> 00:13:10,633
-Yeah? To a big concert?
-...somewhere.

282
00:13:10,699 --> 00:13:13,433
To me, doing stand-up,
it's like being a surfer.

283
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:16,066
You just paddle out
and you catch some waves.

284
00:13:16,133 --> 00:13:17,333
That's why I paint.

285
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:19,800
I am in control, I do it, and I'm done.

286
00:13:19,866 --> 00:13:22,466
And I go,
"Something that wasn't there. Fantastic."

287
00:13:28,266 --> 00:13:30,000
[Jim] Next, Le Mans.

288
00:13:30,066 --> 00:13:32,266
I don't even know if Le Mans
exists anymore.

289
00:13:32,333 --> 00:13:34,300
It does, but you don't say it like that.

290
00:13:34,366 --> 00:13:35,599
"Le Mans."

291
00:13:35,666 --> 00:13:37,666
That's French for "The lemons."

292
00:13:40,699 --> 00:13:42,433
Stupid [bleep] makes me laugh.

293
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:44,433
-Why is that?
-It's not stupid.

294
00:13:48,661 --> 00:13:50,396
So, you're going to show me
the studio today?

295
00:13:50,466 --> 00:13:51,533
[Jim] Yeah, man. Sure.

296
00:13:51,599 --> 00:13:53,400
-Oh, great.
-It's my heart.

297
00:13:53,466 --> 00:13:54,966
I love this place.

298
00:14:06,812 --> 00:14:07,947
[Jerry] Wow.

299
00:14:08,047 --> 00:14:08,981
[Jim] Come on in.

300
00:14:09,048 --> 00:14:11,183
-Thank you so much for showing me this.
-Yeah.

301
00:14:11,250 --> 00:14:13,986
-Well, you're the only one who's seen it.
-I love this.

302
00:14:14,066 --> 00:14:16,033
This is the womb for me.

303
00:14:16,099 --> 00:14:17,666
And I just love paint.

304
00:14:17,733 --> 00:14:19,833
I love the colors,
I love everything about it.

305
00:14:19,892 --> 00:14:23,162
-The gorilla with the machine gun.
-Yeah, the gorilla with the machine gun.

306
00:14:23,233 --> 00:14:24,833
I got power, now.

307
00:14:34,699 --> 00:14:37,033
-Are these canvases?
-Yeah, those are canvases.

308
00:14:37,099 --> 00:14:38,099
Getting big.

309
00:14:38,166 --> 00:14:41,000
Well, if you build it, they will come.

310
00:14:41,066 --> 00:14:42,400
[Jerry] Field of Dreams.

311
00:14:42,533 --> 00:14:43,866
I love that movie.

312
00:14:43,933 --> 00:14:44,933
At the end--

313
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,533
You sob. There's no way not to sob.

314
00:14:46,599 --> 00:14:47,866
There's no way you can't.

315
00:14:47,933 --> 00:14:50,000
That simple thing of like,
"Let's have a catch.

316
00:14:50,066 --> 00:14:52,133
-Spend your time with me for a second."
-Mmm-hmm.

317
00:14:52,199 --> 00:14:53,866
-Is so...
-Right!

318
00:14:53,933 --> 00:14:55,800
...insanely ingrained in us.

319
00:14:55,866 --> 00:14:58,666
That is a great description of why
that moment's so powerful.

320
00:14:58,733 --> 00:15:00,266
Just a moment with me.

321
00:15:00,333 --> 00:15:01,866
-Yeah.
-Just give me a moment.

322
00:15:01,933 --> 00:15:03,900
That's that whole movie,
it comes down to that.

323
00:15:06,666 --> 00:15:08,733
[inaudible]

324
00:15:26,659 --> 00:15:29,128
[Jim] I could disappear
from show business forever,

325
00:15:29,194 --> 00:15:31,864
and feel wonderful about what I've done,

326
00:15:31,933 --> 00:15:33,199
what I've experienced,

327
00:15:33,266 --> 00:15:34,433
what I've been given,

328
00:15:34,500 --> 00:15:37,433
but I am always grateful

329
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:39,800
that wherever I go, somehow,

330
00:15:39,866 --> 00:15:42,866
I did something
that makes people brighten.

331
00:15:49,815 --> 00:15:52,518
And that is a wonderful thing somehow.

332
00:16:03,599 --> 00:16:04,900
That was awesome.

333
00:16:04,966 --> 00:16:06,766
-I need a hug from you.
-[woman] Oh.

334
00:16:06,832 --> 00:16:09,468
-[Jim] Oh, yeah!
-You're my favorite.

335
00:16:09,633 --> 00:16:12,733
I could get addicted to this.
It's so good.

336
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:15,266
I gotta share it. Hold it.

337
00:16:15,333 --> 00:16:17,566
I'm gonna need to let some things out.

338
00:16:17,666 --> 00:16:20,400
Just give it here. Give it to me.

339
00:16:21,099 --> 00:16:23,433
-It was awesome. Did it feel good?
-You are so sexy.

340
00:16:23,482 --> 00:16:26,018
-[all laugh]
-[both] Mmm!

